# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
"""Move 297 port-made box breaks to the end of a sentence.

When one of Capcom's messages is too long for a single DS box, dstext.convert()
splits it into two boxes at a width-balanced word boundary, which can land in
the middle of a sentence ("...should be more" / "than capable of..."). For the
297 messages in this table a sentence end also fits both boxes (240 px, three
lines each, the port's own dstext._layout), so the break is moved there
instead. Only WHERE the break falls changes: the words, their order, and every
control code with its arguments stay exactly as they were and in the same
order relative to the words.

What a moved break looks like, exactly as convert()'s emit() writes one:
  * the old break is removed: its {E102} (or the auto-advance {E108 3C}{E104}),
    the {E107 3} that opened box 2, and, when the old break sat inside a thought
    or a coloured span, the synthetic ")" "(" pair and the synthetic {E040}
    close / re-open pair emit() added there;
  * the new break goes after the sentence's last word and after any plain
    pause/pace codes ({E108}, {E107}) directly following it, which stay at the
    end of box 1; the same break codes are written there, with the ")" "(" pair
    for a thought and the close / re-open pair for a span still open;
  * both boxes are re-laid by dstext._layout: a space unit before a word on
    the same line, a 0x0A where a line wraps, the ")" width reserved.

The messages were chosen by a classifier over the built ROM (only clean
sentence ends and approved special cases; a break next to a presentation code
such as a sound cue or an animation stays where it ships) and reviewed by hand.
A break is also left where it ships when moving it would carry a presentation
code (anything but {E107}, {E108} and the style codes) from one box into the
other, so every such cue still fires in the box it fired in before (15 more
messages kept this way).

Same hash-guard discipline as tools/linefix.py: a string applies only when its
exact unit stream at this module's point in the pipeline still hashes to what
the table was built against, and the rebuilt stream is checked against its own
hash before it ships. Either mismatch leaves the whole string as it was and is
counted as a fallback, one per box break the string would have moved. No game
text is stored here: 'segs' is a list of (start, end) slices of the string's
own current units plus literal structural units only (the space unit, 0x0A,
the two parentheses, and the break and style codes with their arguments).

PIPELINE POINT: tools/inject.py runs this LAST, after tools/skipguard.py, just
before the archive is packed, so every hash is keyed to the final built
string. Three of the moved breaks sit in strings skipguard also edits (9/29
twice, 227/0); skipguard's own hashes are keyed to the unmoved
strings, so running this first would turn those guards into fallbacks. None
of the moved breaks is in the box that holds a guarded entrance, and a moved
break never touches an {E111} or {E112}, so each guard's {E112} keeps its
exact place next to its entrance.

Rows: 297 box breaks in 272 strings (the table key is (entry, string); 'boxes'
lists the index of box 1 of every two-box message moved in that string).
"""
import hashlib
import os
import struct
import sys
sys.path.insert(0, os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__)))
from spt import all_strings, parse, tails as _spt_tails
from build_spt import build_ds
import dstext

